# Immutable binary artefacts — tracked by their companion .md source pages, not directly.
# Keeps the repo lean; images are only ever moved, never edited.
# PDFs are NOT tracked, anywhere, as of 2026-08-17 (documentation/osint-pdf-history-purge.md,
# history rewritten with git-filter-repo same day). Superseded the prior rule below this
# comment used to state ("PDFs in raw/ ARE tracked... do not re-add *.pdf here") — that
# held from Git LFS's retirement (2026-07-29) until the purge. A PDF is never edited after
# capture, so version control bought nothing for it; the backup of record is Bill's
# FreeFileSync/Dropbox mirror, outside this repo, not git. .gitattributes routes no binary
# rule either; since 2026-09-10 it carries one line, `* -text`, which only tells git never
# to convert line endings (see its own header). The budget-archive block at the foot
# untracked its PDFs earlier (2026-08-03) on the same reasoning; this line now generalises
# that to raw/ and new-budget/ too, so that block is redundant but left as the historical
# record.
*.pdf
*.PDF
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp
*.docx
*.xlsx
# ...except where a spreadsheet IS the primary artefact: Benin's estimates volume is an
# .xlsx in all three years, and Burundi's PTBA likewise (post-run note 11, 2026-07-28).
!raw/**/*.xlsx
!budget-archive/**/*.xlsx
!new-budget/**/*.xlsx
*.zip
.env
.git-note
.obsidian/
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/settings.json
.trash/
external-datasets/
my-notes/
run-overnight.log
scratchpad/
# The derived index (scripts/build-index.py). Rebuilt from scratch in ~3s and
# never appended to, so it cannot drift out of step with the vault — only go
# stale, which its consumers detect and repair themselves. Tracking a 27 MB
# artefact rewritten on every rebuild would churn every commit and grow .git
# against PRUNE.md's purpose, for a file nothing reads. It owes CORPUS nothing
# either — CORPUS builds its own index from raw/ and wiki/.
index/
# outputs/reports/ and outputs/catalogue/ retired from git 2026-08-16 (OSINT/
# CORPUS migration, R6) — CORPUS now authors its own reports and catalogue
# from raw/ / wiki/, so OSINT's copies are dead. outputs/non-state-finance/
# and outputs/budgets/ stay tracked: OSINT's own compile, still written every
# ingest-fired FINANCE-COMPILE.
outputs/reports/
outputs/catalogue/
__pycache__/
# Mirror telemetry (2026-08-03, review task 6). Written by `mirror.bat` on its own
# schedule, not on any commit, so tracking it would leave the tree permanently dirty and
# make the `commits` count in the standing tally meaningless. Each file is its own
# history, and both are themselves mirrored to Dropbox and D:\OSINT.
logs/mirror_log.md
logs/mirror-ffs/
# Same reasoning, for stream-progress.py's transcript of the sweep_cycle claude call
# (2026-08-10): overwritten every run, not committed to, so tracking it would leave the
# tree permanently dirty for a file that only ever describes the most recent run.
logs/sweep-console.log
logs/sweep-console.jsonl

# --- the budget archive's SOURCE PDFs are held on disk and backed up outside git ---
# Bill, 2026-08-03, superseding the per-file list this block used to carry. 507 PDFs =
# 5.53 GB, and budget-archive was 3.23 GB of a 4.1 GB .git — 79% of all history weight —
# for no benefit git provides: they are immutable, never diffed, and the backup of record
# is the FreeFileSync flashdrive and the Dropbox mirror, not this repo. Untracked
# 2026-08-03 with `git rm --cached`; the bytes never moved. History still holds them, so
# this stops future growth rather than reclaiming the 3.23 GB — reclaiming that needs a
# rewrite, which is not worth breaking every commit SHA for.
#
# **Only the PDFs.** The archive's text stays tracked and is worth its 111 MB: the 457
# `-companion.md` pages, the 114 extraction CSVs (`BUDGET-EXTRACT.md`'s output — losing
# their history would lose reversibility on the extraction itself), and the 94 `.txt`
# sidecars, which are the only way to grep a document git no longer holds. The 13 .xlsx
# stay too, negated above: post-run note 11 records that for Benin and Burundi the
# spreadsheet IS the primary artefact.
#
# What this means for a fresh clone: it carries the text but not the source PDFs, so
# `BUDGET-EXTRACT.md`'s re-extract only runs on a machine holding the mirror.
# `new-budget/` is deliberately untouched — it is a queue, not an archive, and a staged
# document is work outstanding that should survive in git until it is extracted.
/budget-archive/**/*.pdf

# --- documents too large for git (>90MB), held locally and backed up outside git ---
# Bill, 2026-07-29: not distribution artefacts; the wiki cites them by filename and the
# bytes live on the local disk and its backups. .gitignore cannot express a size rule, so
# the paths are listed here and the 90MB bar itself is enforced by .githooks/pre-commit.
# The fourteen budget-archive entries this list used to carry are covered by the folder
# rule above; only the ones outside it remain.
/raw/2026/2026-04-01 State of Internet Freedom in Zambia 2026 6th Edition.pdf

# The IATI master id list: ~25 MB, rewritten every poll, rebuildable from the API by
# sweep/donor/iati/build-last-poll-ids.py. Versioning it costs ~1 GB of git a year for a
# derived cache; the twice-daily mirror is where its backup guarantee lives (IATI-POLL.md).
sweep/donor/iati/last-poll-ids.txt
/raw/2026/2026-01-07-arpce-algeria-annual-report-2024.pdf

# derived status snapshot, regenerated by scripts/status.py on every `wiki status`
logs/status.md

# The cycle manifest (scripts/cycle-manifest.py) — what CORPUS reads about a run: the
# commit the mirror carries, when collection stopped, and the counts the pass measured.
# Written AFTER the run's final commit, so `head` names the commit that is actually on
# O:\ — a file committed inside that commit could not name it. Same reasoning as
# logs/status.md above: derived, regenerable, and tracking it would leave the tree
# permanently dirty and make the `commits` count meaningless.
# Being gitignored, it is also unrestorable: nothing in history brings it back if it is
# removed. It went missing overnight on 2026-09-07 (notes-for-osint 133) and stopped
# CORPUS's cycle trigger twice. Never run `git clean`, `git stash -u` left unpopped, or
# any other command that discards untracked files in this tree without excluding this
# path and the one below it explicitly.
cycle-manifest.json

# The collection stamp (scripts/cycle-manifest.py --stamp) — sweep_closed/ingest_started/
# last_admission from whichever pass most recently closed ingest, read into the manifest's
# `collection` block above. A single overwritten object, not a log; same reasoning as
# cycle-manifest.json — derived from a measurement already in git (the run's log.md line).
# Same unrestorable risk and the same exclusion rule (notes-for-osint 133).
logs/collection-stamp.json

# The night's per-stage usage buffer (scripts/usage-log.py --stage), read into the manifest's
# `usage` block at the close and emptied by --reset at the next night's first act. A
# working buffer, not a record: logs/usage-log.csv is the record.
logs/usage-stages.jsonl
